## 2026-08-12 -- Svelte frontend: canvas, palette, outline panel: build order step 8

The frontend (`web/frontend`, Svelte 5 + Vite + Svelte Flow) was already scaffolded on this branch --
Shell/CanvasNode/Palette/OutlinePanel/Toolbar components, `state.svelte.js`, and the `api.js`/`ws.js`
clients were all present and wired together, just uncommitted and unverified. This session ran the whole
loop end to end on the VM (drag a node from the palette, watch it go pending -> up as the reconciler
creates the real podman container, connect two nodes into a veth link, delete via the outline panel's
hover action and via canvas Backspace) and fixed the two bugs that surfaced during that verification.

### Fixed

**`web/frontend/src/lib/state.svelte.js`'s `genId`** -- used `crypto.randomUUID()`, which browsers restrict
to secure contexts. Loading the app over plain HTTP from a LAN IP (the VM's actual deployment shape, not
`localhost`) left `crypto.randomUUID` undefined, so dragging any node onto the canvas threw and silently
did nothing. Switched to `crypto.getRandomValues`, which carries no such restriction.

**`web/frontend/src/lib/api.js`'s `getTopology`/`putTopology`** -- the backend marshals a nil Go slice as
JSON `null`, so an empty topology round-trips as `{"nodes":null,"links":null}` rather than `{"nodes":[],...}`.
`OutlinePanel.svelte` reads `topology.nodes.length` directly rather than via the `?? []` guard used
everywhere else in this codebase, so the first document load crashed the canvas before a single node could
be placed. Added a `normalizeTopologyDoc` helper so every document leaving `api.js` already has
array-typed `nodes`/`links`, instead of relying on each consumer to remember the guard.

### Changed

**`CLAUDE.md`** -- checked off build order step 8.

### Verified manually on the VM

Dragged all four node types (host, router, switch, internet/NAT edge) onto the canvas and watched each
reconcile from a dashed pending outline to a solid green up border as podman/netlink created the real
container; connected two nodes into a link that came up solid immediately after; deleted a node from the
outline panel's hover "x" and confirmed its touching link went with it; deleted a node via canvas
selection + Backspace. `podman ps` / `ip netns` were empty again after each teardown. `go test ./...` and
`npm run build` both stayed green throughout.

## 2026-08-11 -- Traffic-stats polling + `traffic_stats` push: build order step 7

The reconciler now samples real kernel byte counters off every up link once a
second and pushes the deltas over the same topology WebSocket the frontend
will use for the moving-dot animation. Validated on the VM: a two-host link
carrying a `ping` flood produced matching forward and reverse deltas on the
`/ws` connection at roughly 1Hz, alongside the existing `topology_status`
pushes, with the flood's own packet loss at 0% throughout.

### Added

**`internal/driver/driver.go`** -- new `LinkCounters` type (`TxBytes`,
`RxBytes`, cumulative kernel counters) and a `Driver.LinkCounters(ctx, owner,
link)` method. It only reads endpoint 0's veth end: a veth pair's two ends
are one physical wire, so end 0's `TxBytes`/`RxBytes` already carry both
directions (`TxBytes` out to endpoint 1, `RxBytes` in from endpoint 1) --
there is no need to open endpoint 1's netns at all.

**`internal/driver/podman/netlink.go`** -- implements `LinkCounters` by
opening endpoint 0's node netns (the same `openNodeNetns`/`netlink.Handle`
path `CreateLink`/`UpdateNodeConfig` already use), finding the link's veth
by its deterministic `driver.VethName`, and reading `Attrs().Statistics`.

**`internal/driver/fake/fake.go`** -- a `counters` map plus `LinkCounters`
(returns the last value set, or an injected failure) and `SetLinkCounters`,
so reconciler tests can drive synthetic traffic between poll ticks without
touching real kernel state.

**`internal/reconcile/traffic.go`** -- the poller itself. `TrafficDelta`
(`linkId`, `direction` -- `fwd` for `Endpoints[0]->[1]`, `rev` for the
reverse -- `bytesDelta`) is the wire payload. `Engine.pollTraffic` walks
every link the last reconcile cycle reported `up`, reads its counters,
diffs against a per-link baseline kept on the `Run` goroutine (no lock
needed -- only that goroutine ever touches it, same as every other
podman/netlink call), and publishes non-zero deltas as one batch via a new
`Engine.SubscribeTraffic`. A link's first sighting only establishes a
baseline (never a delta -- there is nothing to diff yet, and diffing against
zero would report a link's entire lifetime traffic as one spike). A link
that disappears from the desired topology has its baseline dropped, so a
reused ID does not read as a huge negative delta against a stale reading. A
counter that goes backwards (interface recreated between polls) is treated
as a fresh baseline rather than underflowing. `Engine.Run` gained a second
ticker (`TrafficInterval`, 1s) alongside the existing debounce timer.

### Changed

**`internal/httpapi/ws.go`** -- `handleWS` now also subscribes via
`SubscribeTraffic` and pushes `msgTrafficStats` frames from a second
`select` case. Traffic and status are deliberately two separate
subscriptions (as the doc comment already called for) so a slow consumer
dropping frames on one never affects the other.

**`CLAUDE.md`** -- checked off build order step 7.

### Deferred

Frontend consumption of `traffic_stats` (the moving-dot animation itself)
is step 8's Svelte work, not this one. This step only had to prove the
backend samples and pushes real deltas, which the VM ping-flood test above
confirms.
## 2026-08-11 -- Extend adapters to router/switch/edge: build order step 6

All four v1 node types (host, switch, router, edge) are now real containers
with real networking, not just schema entries. Manually validated end to end
on the VM: a switch-bridged pair pings at ttl=64 (proper L2, no routing), and
two hosts on different subnets ping through a router at ttl=63 with a static
route installed (`ip route` showed the connected routes plus the configured
192.0.2.0/24 route, `ip_forward` read back as `1`). Edge nodes create and
link like any other node; NAT/iptables for edge is explicitly not done yet
(see Deferred below), matching how OSPF/BGP and Open vSwitch are already
called out as terminal-tab/v2 work rather than silently missing pieces.

### Fixed

**`internal/driver/podman/netlink.go`** -- `UpdateNodeConfig` applied
node-level config (`ipForward`, `routes`) *before* the per-interface IP loop.
A route with only a gateway (no explicit link index) needs the kernel to
already have a connected route to that gateway, which only exists once the
interface's address is assigned -- so the very first router test failed with
"network is unreachable" even though the interface config that would have
fixed it was sitting a few lines further down, never reached because the
route application returned an error first. Reordered so interface addresses
land before `ipForward`/`routes`. Caught by manually standing up a
host-router-host topology on the VM and reading back `ip route` inside the
router's netns instead of trusting the reconciler's own "up" status.

### Added

**`internal/driver/podman/netlink.go`** -- `ensureBridgeMember` attaches a
switch node's veth ends to a per-netns kernel bridge (`hvbr0`), created
lazily the first time a switch node gets a link; called from `CreateLink`
whenever an endpoint's node type is `switch`. This is structural, not a hot
field: the spec treats switch behavior as "Linux bridge, kernel-native" with
no GUI-configurable fields in v1, so there is nothing for `UpdateNodeConfig`
to do for switches. `setIPForward` flips `net.ipv4.ip_forward` inside a
node's netns by joining it on a locked OS thread (the sysctl lives under
`/proc`, not rtnetlink, so this can't go through the same handle-based calls
as everything else). `UpdateNodeConfig` gained handling for `node.Config`'s
`ipForward` (bool) and `routes` (list of `{dest, via}` CIDR/IP pairs, applied
via `RouteReplace`) -- the router's two hot fields per spec section 7.

### Changed

**`internal/driver/podman/podman.go`** -- `CreateNode` no longer rejects
non-host node types. It was already fully generic (image, labels, sleep
infinity, no network at create time); the only thing gating it to hosts was
an explicit early return left over from build order step 4. Removed the
gate and rewrote the doc comment to say plainly that all four v1 types are
identical plain containers at this layer, and that type-specific behavior
lives entirely in `CreateLink` (switch bridging) and `UpdateNodeConfig`
(router's `ipForward`/`routes`).

**`cmd/hivenet/driver_linux.go`** -- updated `driverName` and the doc
comment above `newDriver` to say "steps 4-6" instead of "steps 4-5", since
this file's whole point is to be the one place that names which build order
steps the active driver actually covers.

### Deferred

Edge nodes get NAT/masquerading and a default route out in the spec (section
7), but that needs either shelling into the container or an nftables/iptables
Go library, both a meaningfully bigger and riskier change than the rest of
this step. Left undone for now rather than rushed in; an edge node today is
just a plain container with whatever interfaces and IPs you give it, same as
a host.


# Hivenet changelog

Every change to code, schema, dependencies or build process gets an entry here,
written in the same session as the change. Newest first. Record the *why* —
that's the part a diff can't recover later.

## 2026-08-11 -- Netlink adapter: build order step 5

Two hosts now ping each other end to end. `CreateLink` builds a veth pair in
the Hivenet process's own netns, moves one end into each endpoint node's netns
(the symlinks step 4 left under /var/run/netns), and tags each end with the
link's JSON as its kernel alias so `ReadActual` can reconstruct link geometry
after a crash with no in-memory cache (CLAUDE.md, "Never trust in-memory state as ground truth"). `DestroyLink` searches every owner node's netns for the
aliased end rather than trusting a caller-supplied endpoint, since after a
crash nothing but the kernel's own state can be trusted. `UpdateNodeConfig`
applies per-interface IP/CIDR live via `AddrReplace`, keyed off the same veth
alias. Manually validated on this VM: PUT a two-host-one-link topology with
day-one IP config, confirmed both veth ends carried the real addresses (not
just link-local), pinged h1 to h2 with 0% loss, removed the link and watched
both netns drop back to just `lo`, then destroyed the nodes and confirmed
`podman ps` and `/var/run/netns` were empty again.

### Fixed

**`internal/reconcile/diff.go`** -- a node created (or recreated) in the same
`Diff()` call that also set its day-one hot config never actually got that
config applied: both the "node doesn't exist" and "cold field changed, node is
being recreated" branches ended in `continue` before the `hotChange` check
further down ever ran, so an interface IP submitted alongside a brand-new node
silently sat unconfigured until some later, unrelated write happened to nudge
the reconciler again. Caught by manually creating h1/h2 with IP config in the
same PUT that created them and finding no IPv4 address on either veth end.
Added `needsConfigApply`, which checks the desired node's own Config and
interface Configs directly instead of diffing against a zero-value Node --
diffing against zero would also flag the bare structural interface entries
CreateLink is responsible for as "changed," which is what the first, reverted
attempt at this fix did and why it broke five existing table-driven cases.
Two new cases cover both branches: a fresh node with day-one config, and a
cold-recreated node with config on the new side.

### Added

**`internal/driver/podman/netlink.go`** -- the netlink half of `driver.Driver`
(spec section 11 item 5): `CreateLink`/`DestroyLink` for veth pairs and netns
moves, `UpdateNodeConfig` for live per-interface IP, and
`readNetnsLinksBestEffort` which decodes the alias podman.go's `ReadActual`
now folds into every node's link set. `go.mod` picks up
`github.com/vishvananda/netlink` and `github.com/vishvananda/netns` for this.

### Changed

**`internal/driver/podman/podman.go`** -- `ReadActual` composes nodes (from
container labels, as before) with links (from `readNetnsLinksBestEffort`
against each node's netns) into one `topology.Topology`. `CreateNode` now
also calls `ensureNetnsSymlink` so `CreateLink` has something under
/var/run/netns to address, and `DestroyNode` removes that symlink so a
leftover entry doesn't outlive its container and leak forever. `CreateLink`,
`DestroyLink`, and `UpdateNodeConfig` moved out of this file entirely, in
favor of the real implementations in netlink.go.

**`cmd/hivenet/driver_linux.go`** -- `driverName` no longer says netlink is
unimplemented; it names both adapters and the build order steps they cover.

**`CLAUDE.md`** -- build order steps 4 and 5 marked done, with a short note on
how each was validated on the VM.

---

## 2026-08-10 -- Podman adapter: build order step 4

Go was upgraded from not-installed to 1.22.2 via apt, then to 1.26.5 via
snap once go.mod needed 1.23+ for the podman v5 bindings (CLAUDE.md,
"Toolchain"). The `go` directive in go.mod is now 1.25.0 (bumped
automatically by `go get` when it resolved podman/v5's own requirement).
`github.com/coder/websocket` was unpinned from v1.8.13 to v1.8.15 per the
same note.

### Fixed

**`cmd/hivenet/main.go`** -- `run` was declared with its name wrapped in
backticks, a syntax error that failed every build. Fixed to a plain
`func run(...)`.

### Added

**`internal/driver/podman`** -- the real `driver.Driver` implementation for
host nodes (spec section 11 item 4): `CreateNode`/`DestroyNode` launch and
remove a podman container with `--network=none` (spec section 6). The full
`topology.Node` is JSON-encoded onto a `hivenet.node` label so `ReadActual`
can reconstruct it with no in-memory cache -- verified end to end on this
VM by creating a node, killing the hivenet process, and restarting it: the
log showed "adopted running topology nodes=1" and the container's uptime
was untouched, i.e. it was not recreated. `CreateLink`/`DestroyLink` return
a clear "not implemented yet" error until netlink lands in step 5; only
`topology.TypeHost` is accepted for now (step 6 covers switch/router/edge).

**`cmd/hivenet/driver_linux.go`** -- now wires `podman.New` instead of the
fake driver on Linux; `driverName` updated to say so.

### Changed

**`Makefile`** -- added `GOTAGS := containers_image_openpgp` and applied
`-tags $(GOTAGS)` to build/run/test/vet. Without it, linking
`containers/podman/v5` pulls in go.podman.io/image's GPGME-based signature
checker (`github.com/proglottis/gpgme`), which needs cgo; with
CGO_ENABLED=0 that package has zero buildable files and the link fails
outright. The tag switches go.podman.io/image to its pure-Go OpenPGP
mechanism instead, which is upstream's own escape hatch for this exact
situation.

### Verified on the VM

Installed `golang-go` (1.22.2) via apt, then `go` (1.26.5) via snap,
symlinking `/usr/local/bin/go` to the snap binary so it wins on PATH.
podman 4.9.3 with an active podman.socket was already present. `make
build`, `make check` (vet + test) and `make run` all pass. Manually drove
the HTTP API (`PUT /api/topology`) to create and destroy a host node and
confirmed with `podman ps` that a real container came up and was torn
down, plus the crash-recovery check described above.

## 2026-08-07 — Project scaffold: build order steps 1–3

First code in the repo. Covers steps 1, 2 and 3 of spec §11; the topology model,
store, reconciler, HTTP/WebSocket layer and test suite all exist and run.
No podman or netlink code yet — those are steps 4–6 and need the Linux VM.

### Added

**`CLAUDE.md`** — working reference for agents: repo layout, commands, the
architecture invariants most likely to be broken by a reasonable-looking change,
the networking gotchas from spec §6, and what is deliberately out of scope.

**`Makefile`** — `make check` (vet + test + fmt) is the gate before calling work
done. Sets `CGO_ENABLED=0` and `GOTOOLCHAIN=local`; see "Toolchain" below for why
those aren't optional.

**`internal/topology`** — the authoritative desired-state document (spec §4).
Nodes, interfaces, links, version counter, deep clone, and validation that
reports every problem at once with a field path per error so the UI can mark the
offending field rather than showing one long string.

Validation enforces the invariants the lower layers depend on:
- IDs are unique and restricted to a path-safe charset — they end up in netns
  symlink paths and podman labels.
- Interface names cap at 15 chars (kernel `IFNAMSIZ`), rejected here where a
  useful error can be shown rather than by netlink later.
- An interface belongs to at most one link, because a veth end can only live in
  one pair.

**`internal/store`** — in-memory topology store plus the separate client-owned
layout store, kept apart as spec §4 requires. Layout writes never bump the
topology version and never trigger a reconcile.

**`internal/reconcile`** — typed ops, the diff, and the debounce/coalesce engine
(spec §5). The engine goroutine is the single writer for all driver state.

**`internal/driver`** — the privileged seam. Interface only; no podman or
netlink imports anywhere in the package. Also holds `VethName` (short hash, 13
chars, so a long link ID can't blow the `IFNAMSIZ` cap) and the netns path
helpers.

**`internal/driver/fake`** — in-memory driver. Backs the whole test suite and
every non-Linux dev session.

**`internal/httpapi`** — REST + WebSocket + embedded frontend on one server.
`GET/PUT /api/topology`, `GET/PUT /api/layout`, `GET /api/status`,
`POST /api/reconcile`, `GET /ws`, `GET /ws/terminal` (501 until step 10),
`/healthz`, and SPA-fallback static serving.

**`internal/auth`** — the multi-tenancy seam from spec §8: an `Authenticator`
interface with a no-op default that returns the constant owner. Middleware only;
no sessions, no user management, no RBAC.

**`web/`** — `go:embed` wrapper plus a placeholder `dist/index.html`. The
placeholder is not decoration: `go:embed` fails the build on a pattern that
matches nothing, so `dist/` must never be empty. It also renders live
`topology_status` pushes, which is how the backend was verified end to end
before any Svelte exists.

### Decisions worth recording

**Adopt-on-start instead of reconcile-on-start.** The store is in-memory, so
booting with an empty desired state and reconciling would tear down a lab that
was running perfectly well — a restart of the app would be a restart of the lab.
`Engine.Adopt` instead seeds the store from actual state when the store is
untouched. Revisit when persistence lands.

**Cold fields are exactly `type` and `image`.** Everything else is hot and
applies live via `UpdateNodeConfig`, matching spec §7 ("all four adapters expose
only hot fields via GUI for v1"). Encoded in one place, `reconcile/diff.go`.

**Recreating a node rebuilds its links, even unchanged ones.** A veth end lives
inside the node's netns and dies with the container, so a node recreate that
left its links alone would produce a silently half-wired node. This is the
subtlest thing in the diff and it has a dedicated test.

**Links are unordered pairs.** A–B and B–A are the same wire. Treating them as
distinct would tear down and rebuild every link on every cycle.

**Config compares via canonical JSON, not `reflect.DeepEqual`.** Config values
arrive both from JSON decoding (numbers become `float64`) and from Go literals
in driver code (`int`). Deep-equal would see a phantom diff on every cycle and
rewrite config forever.

**Verify by re-reading after apply.** Each cycle re-reads actual state and
re-diffs rather than assuming its ops took. That catches an op which reported
success without changing anything, and it's what distinguishes a real failure
from drift.

**Version doubles as an optimistic-concurrency check.** `PUT /api/topology` with
a non-zero `version` that doesn't match current returns 409 rather than
clobbering a change the client never saw. Send `version: 0` to skip the check.
Identical writes don't bump the version, so a chatty frontend can't cause a
reconcile storm.

**`owner` is threaded everywhere now**, always `topology.DefaultOwner` in v1.
Zero cost today; expensive to retrofit later (spec §8).

### Dependencies

- `github.com/coder/websocket` **v1.8.13** (only direct dependency).

Two notes. Spec §3 names `nhooyr.io/websocket`; that module was handed to Coder
and now lives at `github.com/coder/websocket` — same library and API, still
maintained, so the new path is used. And it's pinned to v1.8.13 rather than
latest because v1.8.14+ requires Go 1.23, which isn't installed. Unpin after the
Go upgrade.

### Toolchain problems found

**Go 1.21.6 can't produce runnable binaries on this macOS.** Its external linker
omits the `LC_UUID` load command and macOS 15+ refuses to load the result
(`dyld: missing LC_UUID load command`). Anything importing `net/http` picks up
the cgo resolver and hits it — the package builds, then won't run.

`CGO_ENABLED=0` avoids it entirely and is the correct setting regardless, since
spec §2 wants a single static binary and nothing in the dependency tree needs
cgo. The Makefile sets it. One real consequence: **`go test -race` can't run on
`internal/httpapi`**, because race requires cgo. Every other package passes
under `-race`.

**Step 4 is blocked on a Go upgrade.** The podman v5 bindings need Go ≥1.23.
Upgrade the dev machine and the VM together, bump the `go` directive, and unpin
the websocket dependency; that also retires the `LC_UUID` workaround.

### Verified

- `make check` passes: `go vet` clean, `gofmt` clean, all tests green.
- All packages except `internal/httpapi` also pass under `-race` (see above).
- Server run end to end against the fake driver: `PUT` a 2-host + 1-link
  topology → reconcile applied `CreateNode h1`, `CreateNode h2`, `CreateLink l1`
  in phase order → status reported all three `up` at version 1.
- Removing a node and link produced `DestroyLink l1`, `DestroyNode h2`,
  `UpdateNodeConfig h1` — destroy-links-before-nodes and config-last, as spec
  §5.3 requires.
- Invalid topology returned 422 with per-field details
  (`nodes[0].type`, `nodes[0].image`).
- WebSocket delivered the state snapshot on connect and pushed an update on the
  next change.
- SPA fallback serves the shell for unknown paths; `/ws/terminal` returns 501.

### Not done

Build order steps 4–11: podman adapter, netlink adapter, router/switch/edge
adapters, traffic-stats polling, the Svelte frontend, node detail panel,
terminal, and the visual polish pass. Steps 4–6 cannot be developed or tested on
macOS and need the Ubuntu VM with a podman socket.

### Placeholder to change

The Go module path is `github.com/bitcrafttech/hivenet`, guessed from the git
config. If the repo lands elsewhere, change it in `go.mod` and update imports.
